WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.925
How does injection moulding work?

2
00:00:03.475 --> 00:00:06.192
This machine makes plastic bottle caps.

3
00:00:06.741 --> 00:00:09.411
It runs the same round again and again.

4
00:00:10.311 --> 00:00:12.378
On the left is the clamp unit.

5
00:00:12.928 --> 00:00:16.678
It slides the moving half of the mould against the fixed half,

6
00:00:16.878 --> 00:00:19.270
and holds them shut with great force.

7
00:00:20.170 --> 00:00:22.376
On the right is the injection unit.

8
00:00:22.926 --> 00:00:25.678
Pellets from the hopper melt in a heated barrel,

9
00:00:25.877 --> 00:00:29.302
and a screw injects the hot melt into the closed mould.

10
00:00:30.202 --> 00:00:33.221
Inside the mould are hollow shapes of the cap.

11
00:00:33.771 --> 00:00:37.930
The melt fills them and cools, until the cap is stiff.

12
00:00:38.830 --> 00:00:44.489
Then the mould opens, pins push the caps out, and they drop into the bin.

13
00:00:45.039 --> 00:00:48.351
The mould closes, and the round starts again.

14
00:00:49.551 --> 00:00:55.698
That round has six steps: close, inject, hold, cool, open and eject.

15
00:00:56.248 --> 00:01:00.256
Together they make the cycle time, here 30 seconds.

16
00:01:01.156 --> 00:01:05.208
The injection itself takes only 1.5 seconds.

17
00:01:05.758 --> 00:01:09.102
The cooling time is 15 seconds: half the cycle.

18
00:01:10.302 --> 00:01:14.040
Each cavity in the mould is a space in the shape of the part,

19
00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:16.202
and makes one cap per shot.

20
00:01:16.752 --> 00:01:18.203
This mould has 2.

21
00:01:19.103 --> 00:01:21.286
It runs at 20 seconds.

22
00:01:21.836 --> 00:01:25.029
An hour has 3,600 seconds.

23
00:01:25.578 --> 00:01:31.958
Divided by 20, times 2 cavities, that is 360 caps per hour.

24
00:01:33.158 --> 00:01:34.447
How does a part cool?

25
00:01:34.997 --> 00:01:39.074
Water flows through the cooling channels, holes in the steel,

26
00:01:39.274 --> 00:01:40.992
and carries the heat away.

27
00:01:41.543 --> 00:01:44.039
The plastic sets from the outside in.

28
00:01:44.939 --> 00:01:46.901
Plastic conducts heat poorly.

29
00:01:47.451 --> 00:01:50.795
So twice as thick means roughly four times the cooling.

30
00:01:51.345 --> 00:01:55.214
That is how one thick boss, a solid stud for a screw,

31
00:01:55.414 --> 00:01:57.446
sets the cycle of the whole part.

32
00:01:58.646 --> 00:01:59.737
An example.

33
00:02:00.287 --> 00:02:03.515
This cap cools for 12 of its 20 seconds.

34
00:02:04.065 --> 00:02:09.699
The thickest wall is 3 millimetres, and the designer brings it down to 2.4.

35
00:02:10.599 --> 00:02:14.674
Cooling time scales with the square: times 0.64.

36
00:02:15.224 --> 00:02:20.881
It drops to about 7.7 seconds, the cycle to 15.7,

37
00:02:21.081 --> 00:02:26.654
and output rises from 360 to 459 caps per hour.

38
00:02:27.204 --> 00:02:28.539
On the same machine.

39
00:02:29.739 --> 00:02:33.852
A temperature control unit, a cabinet beside the press,

40
00:02:34.052 --> 00:02:37.593
heats or cools the water and pumps it through the channels.

41
00:02:38.493 --> 00:02:41.117
That is how it holds the mould temperature.

42
00:02:41.667 --> 00:02:45.269
For PA6 that is 80 degrees, for example.

43
00:02:45.819 --> 00:02:48.048
Too cold gives a dull surface.

44
00:02:48.948 --> 00:02:52.201
The water must flow hard enough for turbulent flow:

45
00:02:52.201 --> 00:02:54.625
swirling instead of sliding in layers.

46
00:02:55.175 --> 00:02:57.915
Swirling water carries far more heat away.

47
00:02:58.815 --> 00:03:01.731
The part may come out at the ejection temperature:

48
00:03:01.731 --> 00:03:06.106
then its thickest section is stiff enough to be pushed out without bending.

49
00:03:07.306 --> 00:03:10.394
More output can also come from more cavities.

50
00:03:10.944 --> 00:03:16.145
A 4-cavity mould at 20 seconds makes 720 caps per hour.

51
00:03:16.696 --> 00:03:23.388
An 8-cavity one runs slower, at 24 seconds, but makes 1,200.

52
00:03:24.288 --> 00:03:28.677
Every cavity must fill at the same time and pressure: fill balance.

53
00:03:29.227 --> 00:03:32.826
An H-pattern gives each cavity an equal flow path.

54
00:03:33.726 --> 00:03:37.906
In a hot runner the plastic stays molten right up to the cavities.

55
00:03:38.456 --> 00:03:40.309
A valve gate goes further:

56
00:03:40.309 --> 00:03:44.656
a needle in each nozzle opens and closes the gate on command.

57
00:03:45.556 --> 00:03:50.969
A family mould makes different parts in one shot, such as a lid and a base.

58
00:03:51.519 --> 00:03:53.841
Handy, but they rarely fill alike.

59
00:03:55.041 --> 00:03:56.782
What does one cap cost?

60
00:03:57.332 --> 00:04:02.118
First the material: 40 grams of PP, at two euros a kilo.

61
00:04:02.668 --> 00:04:06.929
With a scrap rate of 3 percent that is just over eight cents.

62
00:04:07.829 --> 00:04:08.897
Then the machine.

63
00:04:09.447 --> 00:04:14.149
The machine hour rate is 60 euros: what an hour of press time costs.

64
00:04:14.699 --> 00:04:20.131
At 85 percent efficiency the press makes 408 good parts an hour:

65
00:04:20.131 --> 00:04:22.338
almost fifteen cents each.

66
00:04:23.238 --> 00:04:24.318
Then the mould.

67
00:04:24.868 --> 00:04:27.719
The mould amortisation spreads the eighty

68
00:04:27.719 --> 00:04:30.849
thousand euros it costs over a million parts:

69
00:04:30.849 --> 00:04:32.031
eight cents each.

70
00:04:32.931 --> 00:04:35.311
Together about thirty-one cents.

71
00:04:35.861 --> 00:04:38.299
Machine time is the biggest line here,

72
00:04:38.499 --> 00:04:41.564
so every second of cycle shows up in the price.
